\f0\fs24 \cf2 As soon as you step into the Bevilacqua Room, one thing immediately stands out: everything has remained astonishingly true to the original. There are a few small variations, of course, but the atmosphere is exactly what the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers envisioned more than a century ago.\
One of the most captivating details? The chandelier. What you see today isn\'92t just a decorative choice, it\'92s an erudite reference: it faithfully replicates the model depicted by Vittore Carpaccio in 1495, in the scene 
\f1\i The King of Mauritania Dismisses the Ambassadors
\f0\i0 , part of the famous 
\f1\i Legend of Saint Ursula
\f0\i0  cycle, now housed in the Gallerie dell\'92Accademia in Venice.\
Soft lines, wrought iron, gracefully curved arms holding candle-shaped lamps: a simple object, yet exquisitely elegant. And behind it lies an even more compelling story. In the late 19th century, Carpaccio was rediscovered and became a true style icon for the newly unified Italy. The Savoy monarchs were especially fond of his painted furnishings, seeing them as ideal models for shaping a unified Italian artistic identity.\
And the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers? They were no different. They too drew inspiration from Carpaccio in furnishing their home. Because here, every object is more than just an aesthetic choice, it\'92s a heartfelt tribute to history and to art.\
